\mine{How did major consolidation events (exits, acquisitions and mergers) reshape concentration in India's scheduled domestic passenger aviation market between 2015 and 2026 once common ownership is taken into account, and has the oligopoly narrowed into a durable asymmetric duopoly, judged against pre-defined thresholds?}

\textbf{O1.} Track monthly market structure with three concentration series (brand \HHI{}, ownership \HHI{} and modified \HHI{}), \CRtwo{} and the effective number of firms, and estimate the step change at each exit and merger. The gap between the series shows how much brand-level counting understates concentration. Claims are limited to market structure, as no fare data is available.\\
\textbf{O2.} Measure which airline groups absorbed the passengers and seat capacity of Jet Airways (2019) and Go First (2023) after they exited, and explain the shifts with slot and fleet evidence. Assess the Tata Group consolidation (2022--2024) against CCI's combination order.\\
\textbf{O3.} Assess whether airlines entering after 2015 became persistent competitors.\\
\textbf{O4.} Test the market against explicit duopoly thresholds for \CRtwo{}, \HHI{}, share gap and fringe share. Check whether national figures hide route-level monopolies on the top 20--30 routes where city-pair data allows, and measure resilience through the capacity share where IndiGo is the sole or dominant operator and rivals' spare capacity in December 2025.", "oldString": "\begin{draft}
