Confounding trends (O2):} Akasa Air's ramp-up, SpiceJet's decline and the exiting airlines' own decline before exit overlap the windows, so not all of each change is caused by the exit. Addressed with 6-month windows and a Jet pre-window ending in December 2018.}
    \item \mine{\textbf{Small sample (O2):} two exits and two consolidation steps allow no significance tests. The decomposition is an exact accounting identity, and results are checked on both passengers and seat-km.}
    \item \mine{\textbf{Reverse causality (O2):} rivals' growth may have helped cause the exits, so gains are described as redistribution, not as effects of the exit.}
    \item \mine{\textbf{Different market definitions (O2)

[\mine{\textbf{H4a.}}] \mine{(O2) \textbf{Exit absorption:} We expect IndiGo's absorption ratio to be the largest of any group after both exits, and larger after Go First than after Jet Airways.}
    \item[\mine{\textbf{H4b.}}] \mine{(O2) \textbf{Exit-driven concentration:} We expect IndiGo's gain to account for more than half of the rise in \HHI{} after each exit.}
    \item[\mine{\textbf{H4c.}}] \mine{(O2) \textbf{Tata consolidation:} We expect the observed rise in \HHI{} at each Tata step to be at least the mechanical rise from common ownership alone.}
