\mine{Ownership stakes for \MHHI{}: company filings and CCI combination orders (Tata, SIA and AirAsia Berhad stakes in Vistara and AirAsia India, with dates)} & \mine{Downloaded PDFs, pages recorded} & \mine{O1 (\MHHI{})} & \mine{\cellbullet{Stakes changed over time, so dates must be verified.}\par\cellbullet{Control rights are assumed proportional to equity unless filings show otherwise.}\par} \\
\hline
\mine{Combination Registration No.\ C-2022/05/933: Acquisition and subsequent merger of AIX Connect (AirAsia India) with Air India Express, and No.\ C-2023/04/1022: Merger of Tata SIA Airlines (Vistara) into Air India Limited} & \mine{cci.gov.in, Combination orders; PDFs downloaded, order numbers and approval dates recorded} & \mine{O1 (dates when brands join the Air India Group)} & \mine{\cellbullet{Approval date may precede operational integration, so it is used only as the dated boundary for group membership.}\par\cellbullet{Order number C-2022/05/933 to be verified.}\par}

\mine{(O1) \textbf{Lasting steps:} We expect each of the four consolidation events to produce a positive and lasting step in ownership \HHI{} ($\gamma_k > 0$ and significant).}
    \item \mine{(O1) \textbf{Grouping effect:} We expect ownership \HHI{} to exceed brand \HHI{} throughout the period, with the gap largest between January 2022 and the December 2024 mergers.}
    \item \mine{(O1) \textbf{Partial ownership:} We expect \MHHI{} to exceed ownership \HHI{} mainly before January 2022, reflecting Tata's stakes in Vistara and AirAsia India, and this gap to be small relative to the grouping effect.}
    \item (O4) After December 2024, every month meets all four duopoly cut-offs.
    \item (O4) Ownership \HHI{} exceeds brand \HHI{}, and the gap widens from January 2022.
    \item[\mine{\textbf{H6a.}}] \mine{(O2) \textbf{Exit absorption:} We expect IndiGo's absorption ratio to be the largest of any group after both exits, and larger after Go First than after Jet Airways.}
    \item[\mine{\textbf{H6b.}}] \mine{(O2) \textbf{Exit-driven concentration:} We expect IndiGo's gain to account for more than half of the rise in \HHI{} after each exit.}
    \item[\mine{\textbf{H6c.}}] \mine{(O2) \textbf{Tata consolidation:} We expect the observed rise in \HHI{} at each Tata step to be at least the mechanical rise from common ownership alone.}

Herfindahl--Hirschman Index and the two-firm concentration ratio, $\HHI{} = \sum_i s_i^2$ and $\CRtwo{} = S_{(1)} + S_{(2)}$, at three levels that differ only in how a firm is defined before squaring:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Tier 1 (Brand-\HHI{}):} each operating brand is a separate firm, as in prior work \parencite{kumari2026soaring}: $\text{Brand-}\HHI{} = \sum_i s_i^2$.
    \item \textbf{Tier 2 (Ownership-\HHI{}):} brands under common control are summed into ownership groups $S_g$ (Section 2) before squaring. The \textbf{grouping effect}, $\text{Ownership-}\HHI{} - \text{Brand-}\HHI{}$, is the concentration hidden by counting commonly controlled brands as rivals.
    \item \textbf{Tier 3 (Modified \HHI{}, \MHHI{}):} adds partial ownership between rival groups, following \textcite{obriensalop2000}:
    \begin{equation}
    \MHHI{} = \text{Ownership-}\HHI{} + \sum_{g}\sum_{h \neq g} w_{gh}\, S_g S_h, \qquad w_{gh} = \frac{\sum_{o} c_{og}\, f_{oh}}{\sum_{o} c_{og}\, f_{og}},
    \end{equation}
