    where $\beta_{mg}$ is owner $m$'s equity stake in group $g$ and $\gamma_{mg}$ its control right, taken as equal to its equity stake. The relevant common owner is Tata, through its stakes in Vistara and AirAsia India before January 2022, verified from company records (Section~3). From January 2022 both are inside the Air India Group, so the term drops out. The difference $\MHHI{} - \text{Ownership-}\HHI{}$ is the \textbf{partial-ownership effect}.
\end{enumerate}

Each month we also report \CRtwo{} and the effective number of firms ($10{,}000/\text{Ownership-}\HHI{}$), and IndiGo's share relative to the second-largest group, $s_{\text{IndiGo}}/S_{(2)}$, as a descriptive measure of asymmetry.

To test whether each event produced a lasting step (H1), we estimate a segmented (interrupted time-series) regression on each of the three index series:
\begin{equation}
\text{Index}_t = \alpha + \beta t + \sum_{k=1}^{4}\gamma_k\,\text{Post}_{kt} + \delta\,\text{COVID}_t + \sum_{m=2}^{12}\mu_m M_{mt} + \varepsilon_t ,
\end{equation}
where $\text{Post}_{kt}$ are the four event indicators and $\text{COVID}_t$ and $M_{mt}$ the controls defined in Section~4. Standard errors use the Newey--West correction with 12 lags \parencite{neweywest1987}. H1 is supported if $\gamma_k > 0$ and significant in the ownership-\HHI{} model; the brand-\HHI{} and \MHHI{} models show whether the steps depend on how firms are counted.

For H2 and H3, we compare the average monthly grouping and partial-ownership effects across three periods: before January 2022, January 2022 to November 2024, and from December 2024.

\textbf{Justification.} Changing only the definition of a firm isolates how much ownership adds to measured concentration, and a segmented regression with seasonal and COVID controls separates event steps from trend in a single national monthly series.
\endgroup"}

