\textit{Stage 1: Market shares.}
\[
s_{gt} = \frac{Q_{gt}}{\sum_j Q_{jt}} \times 100, \qquad \bar s_g^{\,k} = \frac{1}{|W^{k}|}\sum_{t \in W^{k}} s_{gt}
\]
$s_{gt}$ is the share of ownership group $g$ in month $t$, with $Q$ as passengers or seat-km. $\bar s_g^{\,k}$ is its mean over a window of up to 12 months before ($k=\text{pre}$) or after ($k=\text{post}$) an exit, excluding the exit month.

\textit{Stage 2: Who absorbed the exiting share.}
\[
\Delta s_g = \bar s_g^{\,\text{post}} - \bar s_g^{\,\text{pre}}, \qquad a_g = \frac{\Delta s_g}{\bar s_x^{\,\text{pre}}} \times 100
\]
$\Delta s_g$ is the change in share. $a_g$ is the portion of the exiting airline $x$'s share taken by $g$ (all $a_g$ sum to 100\%). Where a group's seat-km ratio exceeds its passenger ratio, indicating capacity-led growth, we check its company filings for slot and aircraft additions \parencite{spicejet2020annual,goair2021drhp}.

\textit{Stage 3: Who drove concentration.}
\[
c_g = 2\,\bar s_g^{\,\text{pre}}\,\Delta s_g + (\Delta s_g)^2, \qquad \Delta\text{HHI} = -\big(\bar s_x^{\,\text{pre}}\big)^2 + \sum_{g \neq x} c_g
\]
$c_g$ is group $g$'s contribution to the change in \HHI{}. $\Delta\text{HHI}$ is the total change after the exit. Comparing each $c_g$ with $\Delta\text{HHI}$ shows which group's gain raised concentration the most after an exit.

\textbf{B.\ Consolidation}

\textit{Stage 1: Effect of common ownership.}
\[
\Delta\text{HHI}^{\text{mech}} = 2\sum_{a<b;\; a,b \in G} s_a\, s_b
\]
$\Delta\text{HHI}^{\text{mech}}$ is the rise in \HHI{} from common ownership alone, for brands $G$ at shares in the month before the event. It is compared with the observed change in ownership \HHI{} at the January 2022 acquisition and in brand \HHI{} at the December 2024 mergers, and the result is discussed against the reasoning in CCI's combination order \parencite{cci2023vistara}.

\textbf{Justification.} Two exits and two consolidation steps are too few for a regression. A before-and-after decomposition shows directly where the share went, the filings explain why, and the CCI order grounds the policy discussion in the regulator's actual reasoning.","newString":"\textbf{Exits.} For each exit, each ownership group's mean share $\bar s_g$ (passengers and seat-km) is compared over up to 12 months before and after, excluding the exit month:
\[
\Delta s_g = \bar s_g^{\,\text{post}} - \bar s_g^{\,\text{pre}}, \qquad a_g = 100\,\frac{\Delta s_g}{\bar s_x^{\,\text{pre}}}, \qquad c_g = 2\,\bar s_g^{\,\text{pre}}\,\Delta s_g + (\Delta s_g)^2 ,
\]
where $x$ is the exiting airline, $a_g$ is the part of $x$'s share absorbed by $g$ (the $a_g$ sum to 100\%), and $c_g$ is $g$'s contribution to $\Delta\HHI{} = -(\bar s_x^{\,\text{pre}})^2 + \sum_{g \neq x} c_g$. Where a group's seat-km absorption exceeds its passenger absorption, its filings are checked for slot and fleet additions \parencite{spicejet2020annual,goair2021drhp}.

\textbf{Consolidation.} At each Tata step, the rise from common ownership alone, $\Delta\HHI{}^{\text{mech}} = 2\sum_{a<b;\,a,b \in G} s_a s_b$ at the previous month's shares, is compared with the observed change (ownership \HHI{} in January 2022, brand \HHI{} in December 2024) and discussed against CCI's combination order \parencite{cci2023vistara}.

\textbf{Justification.} Two exits and two steps are too few for a regression; a before-and-after decomposition shows where share went, filings explain why, and the CCI order grounds the policy discussion."},{"filePath":"/Users/m0c0qiy/Downloads/IISC/IISC Managerial Economics/term-paper/assignment-2/final.tex","oldString":"Track nine entrant brands from their first verified month of scheduled
domestic passenger traffic in the current operating spell through July
2026. Calculate monthly passenger-market shares, compare shares at 12,
24 and 36 elapsed months where observable, and record peak share,
operating duration and final outcome. Missing shares are not treated as zero. Suspensions,
cessation and mergers are verified using public records. Continuing
airlines are right-censored at July 2026. Event-time comparisons align airlines at similar stages of their operating histories, while market-share trajectories, peak share and operating duration help distinguish sustained market presence from short-lived growth. Qualitative evidence from corporate, regulatory and industry sources supplements traffic and survival measures to interpret entrants' observed trajectories.","newString":"Track the nine entrants in event time from their first verified month through July 2026. Compare passenger shares at 12, 24 and 36 months where observable, and record peak share, operating duration and outcome (Table~\ref{tab:variables}). Missing shares are not treated as zero, and continuing airlines are right-censored. Corporate and regulatory records verify suspensions, cessations and mergers and help interpret each trajectory.

\textbf{Justification.} Event time compares airlines at the same stage of their lives, and peak share and duration separate sustained presence from short-lived growth."},{"filePath":"/Users/m0c0qiy/Downloads/IISC/IISC Managerial Economics/term-paper/assignment-2/final.tex","oldString":"\textbf{Concentration and asymmetry (feedback \#2).} Calculate monthly CR2, HHI, share gap and fringe share after grouping airlines under the same owner. Use CR2 $\geq$80\% and fringe share $\leq$20\% as indicators of two-group concentration, adapted from \textcite[p.~290, Table~10.1]{demelo1991industrial}. Fringe share follows directly from CR2, so it is not an independent condition. Use HHI $>$1,800 to identify high concentration \parencite[Sec.~2.1]{dojftc2023merger}. A share gap above 20 percentage points is our cutoff for substantial asymmetry. Check which groups occupy the top two positions and report their individual shares; the thresholds alone do not prove duopoly. Compare ownership HHI with brand HHI to measure how much counting brands separately lowers concentration.

\textbf{Route comparison (feedback \#3).} Apply the same measures to scheduled-flight shares on the selected 20 airport pairs where monthly data is available. Report how many routes exceed the concentration benchmark and compare their results with national figures. Please note that national HHI uses passenger shares, while route HHI uses scheduled-flight-frequency shares.

\textbf{Resilience (feedback \#4).} Report the percentage of sampled routes where IndiGo is the only operator or supplies at least 60\% of scheduled flights. The 60\% threshold is our study-defined exposure flag. Calculate rivals' unused seat-kilometres relative to IndiGo's passenger-kilometres each month, with December 2025 examined separately. This measures the available national capacity buffer; actual accommodation depends on routes and dates.

\textbf{Justification.} Index calculations and comparisons directly answer questions about concentration, ownership and dependence on IndiGo. They suit the available monthly data and keep the conclusions within what passenger totals and approved schedules can establish.","newString":"\textbf{Concentration and asymmetry (feedback \#2).} Each month, the ownership-level market is checked against three cut-offs: \CRtwo{} $\geq$80\% (equivalently, fringe share $\leq$20\%), adapted from \textcite[p.~290, Table~10.1]{demelo1991industrial}; \HHI{} $>$1,800 \parencite[Sec.~2.1]{dojftc2023merger}; and a share gap above 20 percentage points (study-defined). Which groups hold the top two positions is also reported, since the thresholds alone do not prove duopoly.

\textbf{Routes (feedback \#3).} The same measures are applied to scheduled-flight shares on the 20 sampled airport pairs, and the number of routes above the benchmark is compared with national figures.

\textbf{Resilience (feedback \#4).} We report the percentage of sampled routes where IndiGo is the only operator or supplies at least 60\% of flights (study-defined flag), and rivals' spare seat-km relative to IndiGo's RPK, with December 2025 examined separately. This is a national buffer; actual accommodation depends on routes and dates.

\textbf{Justification.} These descriptive comparisons answer the duopoly and dependence questions directly and stay within what passenger totals and approved schedules can establish."}]}

