\subsection{\objref{2}: Exits and Tata consolidation}
Figure~\ref{fig:o2-exits} shows ownership-group passenger shares around the two exits. Neither exit was sudden. Jet Airways' share fell from 16.2\% a year before its April 2019 exit to 5.7\% in the month before, and Go First's fell from 11.1\% to 6.4\% before May 2023, as both airlines grounded aircraft (Table~\ref{tab:o2-decline}). Part of each exiting airline's traffic therefore moved before the exit month, which matters for the before-and-after windows used in Section~5.2. IndiGo shows the clearest rise around both exits, from about 43\% to 48\% after Jet Airways and from about 57\% to 62\% after Go First. The jump in the Air India Group's share in January 2022 is the Tata acquisition grouping Vistara and AirAsia India into the group, not an exit effect.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{o2_exit_share_paths.png}
\caption{Monthly passenger shares by ownership group around the Jet Airways and Go First exits}\label{fig:o2-exits}
\sourcenote{DGCA monthly airline statistics via \textcite{kubicek2026aviationstats}; authors' calculations. Light shading: 12-month pre-exit window; dark shading: post-exit window (10 months for Jet Airways, ending before COVID-19); dashed line: exit month, excluded from both windows.}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Exiting airline's share in the months before its exit (\%)}\label{tab:o2-decline}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Airline & Share of & 12 months before & 6 & 3 & 1 & Exit month \\
\midrule
Jet Airways (Apr 2019) & Passengers & 16.18 & 14.83 & 13.61 & 5.74 & 0.94 \\
 & Seat-km & 16.44 & 14.68 & 12.97 & 5.22 & 0.88 \\
Go First (May 2023) & Passengers & 11.13 & 7.45 & 7.98 & 6.43 & 0.37 \\
 & Seat-km & 10.51 & 7.15 & 7.58 & 6.17 & 0.36 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\sourcenote{DGCA monthly airline statistics via \textcite{kubicek2026aviationstats}; authors' calculations. Jet Airways includes JetLite.}
\end{table}

The market grew in both post-exit windows, so rivals' share gains came with more passengers, not from a shrinking market (Table~\ref{tab:o2-market}). Monthly passengers rose by 4.8\% after Jet Airways and 11.7\% after Go First, while seat-km rose less (4.1\% and 7.2\%), so load factors went up: rivals filled more of their existing seats. The number of groups with at least 1\% share fell by one after each exit.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Market size and capacity before and after each exit (monthly averages)}\label{tab:o2-market}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Exit & Window & Passengers (m) & Seat-km (bn) & Load factor (\%) & Groups $\geq$1\% \\
\midrule
Jet Airways & Apr 2018--Mar 2019 & 11.69 & 13.07 & 86.0 & 8.0 \\
 & May 2019--Feb 2020 & 12.25 & 13.60 & 87.1 & 7.0 \\
Go First & May 2022--Apr 2023 & 11.53 & 13.29 & 84.1 & 5.6 \\
 & Jun 2023--May 2024 & 12.88 & 14.25 & 87.2 & 4.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\sourcenote{DGCA monthly airline statistics via \textcite{kubicek2026aviationstats}; authors' calculations. Load factor is passenger-km divided by seat-km.}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:o2-tata} gives the brand shares that enter the mechanical \HHI{} calculation for each Tata step. The brands brought under one owner in January 2022 held 22.5\% of passengers; those merged in late 2024 held 29.3\%.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Passenger shares of the Tata brands before each consolidation step (\%)}\label{tab:o2-tata}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Step (shares in) & Air India & Air India Express & Vistara & AirAsia India / AIX Connect & Total \\
\midrule
Acquisition, Jan 2022 (Dec 2021) & 8.39 & 0.07 & 7.72 & 6.28 & 22.46 \\
Mergers, Dec 2024 (Sep 2024) & 11.14 & 3.97 & 10.04 & 4.11 & 29.26 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\sourcenote{DGCA monthly airline statistics via \textcite{kubicek2026aviationstats}; authors' calculations. September 2024 is the last month before the AIX Connect merger (October 2024) and the Vistara merger (November 2024).}
\end{table}

