\subsection{\objref{2}: Exits and Tata consolidation}
\textbf{Exits: exploratory analysis.} Neither exit was sudden (Figure~\ref{fig:o2-exits}). Jet Airways' passenger share fell from 16.2\% to 5.7\% in the year before its April 2019 exit, and Go First's from 11.1\% to 6.4\% in the year before its May 2023 exit, as both grounded aircraft. Some traffic therefore moved before the exit month, so the before-window already includes part of the shift and the before-and-after comparison may understate it. IndiGo rose most around both exits, from about 43\% to 48\% after Jet Airways and from about 57\% to 62\% after Go First. The market grew in both post-exit windows, with passengers rising faster than seat-km, so rivals mainly filled existing seats (Table~\ref{tab:o2-market}). Section~5.2 uses these before-and-after windows to measure how much of each exiting airline's share every group absorbed.

